  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sustainable Management of Marine and Coastal Ecosystem Services</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="4">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Sustainable Management of Marine and Coastal Ecosystem Services Project is one of eight core projects that will be implemented under UN Environment&amp;rsquo;s Healthy &amp; Productive Ecosystems (HPE) Subprogramme, and provides an enabling framework for UN Environment to address the ongoing global degradation of coastal and marine ecosystems, and the services that they provide. The ultimate environmental impact that the core project will work towards is &amp;lsquo;coastal and marine ecosystems and habitats are sustainably utilized and rates of degradation are reduced&amp;rsquo; while the development impact is &amp;lsquo;coastal and marine ecosystem services are maintained, enhanced and contribute to societal and where appropriate, economic benefits&amp;rsquo;.
The world&amp;rsquo;s oceans and coasts are under increasing pressure from anthropogenic activities and the resulting adverse impacts. Overfishing, destructive fishing, and other extractive industries, coastal pollution, marine litter, ocean acidification, and warming sea temperatures, among others, are anthropogenic threats that are negatively impacting the resilience of coastal and marine ecosystems, as well as the human societies that depend on them. Delivery of ecosystem goods and services is thus being compromised, making hundreds of millions of coastal inhabitants, especially natural resource-dependent indigenous people, more vulnerable to threats to their lives and livelihoods. Sustainably managing marine and coastal ecosystems so that they remain healthy and continue providing ecosystem goods and services is integral to achieving sustainable development and a more prosperous future for humanity.</narrative>
  </description>
